## Authority
- Read the relevant approved intent, non-goals, boundaries and ownership before editing.
- Treat existing code as evidence, not authority; do not propagate known deviations.
- Preserve scope, contracts and boundaries unless explicitly changed by approved requirements. Escalate unresolved architectural conflicts.

## Ownership
- Find existing owners and extension points before adding implementations.
- Keep domain names consistent with behavior; correct misleading names and comments within scope.
- Centralize shared business semantics, not coincidentally similar syntax.
- Prefer typed contracts and explicit dependencies, state and effects. Isolate required dynamic wiring at discoverable boundaries.
- Extract cohesive responsibilities, not line-count slices. Add abstractions for current needs, not hypothetical reuse.

## Changes
- Inspect definitions, relevant callers and runtime wiring; verify external APIs against referenced versions.
- Make task-bounded but complete changes across callers, registrations, configuration, serialization and tests.
- Fix causes in the owning component; avoid accumulating special-case wrappers.
- Preserve failure, cancellation, transaction and resource-lifetime semantics; never disguise failures as success.
- Remove superseded paths within scope after checking runtime and external consumers; document why required compatibility paths remain.
- Change generation inputs and regenerate outputs; do not hand-edit generated code.

## Verification
- Test requirements and regressions, not implementation details; run repository-required checks for affected targets.
- Do not weaken tests, analyzers, architecture checks or baselines merely to pass.
- Review the diff and affected dependencies against approved intent and boundaries; report unresolved deviations.

## Continuity
- Update affected canonical documentation for approved changes; do not rewrite the intended architecture to justify drift.
- Record verified results, unrun checks and remaining work in existing handoff notes.
